---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const updated = '2026-10-21';
---

<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy" description="How VisaLang researches, writes, and maintains guides." noindex={true} enableAds={false}>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <article class="guide-article narrow-page">
        <p class="eyebrow">Editorial</p>
        <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
        <p class="guide-summary">Last updated: {updated}</p>

        <h2>Our mission</h2>
        <p>VisaLang provides independent, informational guidance to help users identify the language proof to verify for visa, residency, citizenship, study, and work routes. We point users to official sources rather than replacing them; we are not official and do not provide legal or immigration advice.</p>

        <h2>Official-first sourcing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Every guide cites the official exam owner or government authority.</li>
          <li>We use blogs, forums, and third-party materials only for user-language context, never for factual requirements.</li>
          <li>We never invent fees, dates, accepted exams, or policy details.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Source hierarchy and decision checks</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Receiving authority or institution:</strong> the mission, immigration authority, university, employer, or professional body that decides whether proof is needed or accepted.</li>
          <li><strong>Official exam owner:</strong> the provider’s current page for the exact certificate, format, and terms.</li>
          <li><strong>Authorised local test centre:</strong> the current fee, date, ID, cancellation, and result information for the booking you can actually make.</li>
          <li><strong>Independent editorial explanation:</strong> VisaLang explains the decision order and common mistakes, but does not overwrite the sources above.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Before booking or filing, readers must verify their exact case with the local German mission or competent authority and, where relevant, the school, employer, professional body, exam owner, or authorised local test centre.</p>

        <h2>What we do not publish</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Leaked exam questions or copied real exam items.</li>
          <li>Official logos presented in ways that imply endorsement.</li>
          <li>Guaranteed outcomes ("pass for sure", "visa approved").</li>
          <li>Specific fees, dates, or policy numbers without an official source.</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>Editorial method (how a page is made)</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Task definition:</strong> one primary user decision per page; merge pages that share the same task.</li>
          <li><strong>Authority map:</strong> name who decides the route, who owns the exam product, and who runs local booking—before drafting claims.</li>
          <li><strong>Source capture:</strong> record URL, check date, what the source may support, and what it cannot support.</li>
          <li><strong>Original decision help:</strong> add VisaLang-owned structure such as decision trees, checklists, responsibility tables, comparison tables, scenario pitfalls, or reader record templates—without inventing dynamic facts.</li>
          <li><strong>Fail-closed maturity:</strong> starter and verification-pending pages stay outside primary discovery and advertising until a page-specific review closes recorded gaps.</li>
          <li><strong>AI assistance boundary:</strong> drafting and restructuring may use AI tools; factual immigration, exam, fee, seat, and acceptance claims require human source verification against official pages. AI output is never treated as an official source.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>We do not publish invented personal lawyer or immigration-adviser credentials. “Editorial team”, “Source review”, and “Translation review” are work roles.</p>

        <h2>Content update log</h2>
        <p>Substantive deepenings and source-review windows are summarised in the public <a href="/content-update-log/">Content Update Log</a>. A Last Updated date on a guide is not a guarantee that a local fee or seat remains available.</p>
        <h2>Updates and accuracy</h2>
        <p>Every guide shows a Last Updated date. We review high-risk facts such as requirements, fees, dates, centres, accepted certificates, and policy claims against official sources before presenting them. A Last Updated date is not a promise that a time-sensitive fact remains current; readers must re-check before paying or filing. If you spot an error, <a href="/contact/">send the page URL and official source</a>.</p>

        <h2>Responsibility and review labels</h2>
        <p>Guide bylines, source-review status, and review roles come from the guide’s maintained content record. “Editorial team”, “Source review”, and “Translation review” describe work roles, not professional credentials. A pending review label means that no completed review is being claimed.</p>

        <h2>Corrections and affiliate boundary</h2>
        <p>We log a correction, check the source hierarchy above, update the affected page when warranted, and retain a clear official-source reminder. Commercial partners cannot choose a route recommendation, change an acceptance statement, or place a paid option ahead of the official check.</p>

        <h2>Safe preparation</h2>
        <p>We only recommend original practice materials. We do not link to sites selling leaked questions or "guaranteed pass" shortcuts.</p>

        <h2>Commercial content</h2>
        <p>Affiliate links and sponsored prep recommendations appear only <em>after</em> the official-source section on a page, and are clearly labeled. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a>.</p>

        <h2>Independence</h2>
        <p>Editorial decisions are independent of any exam provider, school, or advertiser. No partner can edit our official-source guidance.</p>

        <p class="last-updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
